        "math_academy_id": "q-69752",
        "problem": "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-27x-70}{(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A+B+C.$",
        "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-27x-70}{(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x+3)(4-x)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-27x-70=A(4-x)(x+2)+B(2x+3)(x+2)+C(2x+3)(4-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-27\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})-70 & =A(4-(-\\frac{3}{2}))((-\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)((-\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)(4-(-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{81}{2}-70 & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ -\\frac{55}{4} & =\\frac{11}{4}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{55}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{11} \\\\ A & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(4)^{2}-27(4)-70 & =A(4-4)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(4)+3)(4+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(4)+3)(4-4) \\\\ 112-108-70 & =B(11)(6) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-66}{66} \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-2)^{2}-27(-2)-70 & =A(4-(-2))((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+B(2(-2)+3)((-2)+2) \\\\  & \\,+C(2(-2)+3)(4-(-2)) \\\\ 28+54-70 & =C(-1)(6) \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{-6} \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C=-5+(-1)+(-2)=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
